    "movement": "Poetic Romance & Heartbreak",
    "runtimeMinutes": 128,
    "watchedDate": "2023-11-06",
    "loggedDate": "2023-11-06",
    "rating": 5.0,
    "isRewatch": true,
    "isLiked": true,
    "isDiaryLogged": true,
    "review": "gosh i’m reading this review a year later why did i used to write like such a nerd \n\nyeah man it's magic, this film\n\nthis has now cemented itself as the single greatest movie i've watched. i doubt there'll ever be a movie that i'll attach so strongly to emotionally. a long ride of ups and downs, a beautiful tale of the relationship between mia and sebastian, all laced with some of the most stunning cinematography i've seen in film, along with the most gorgeous soundtrack put to screen. chazelle created something that i don't think anyone else will be able to match for a very long time. \n\nchazelle shows off incredible artistic vision, enabled by cinematographer linus sandgren, resulting in 2 hours of constant beautiful imagery. the colours, composition, and thought behind each scene is powerful. it’s clear that chazelle made no compromises for this film, and not a second of the movie feels wasted or unnecessary. the natural flow of the film makes it an incredibly emotional one, and the storytelling is so realistic but cinematic/dramatic at the same time. many of the interior shots in this film, especially the ones when the actor’s ’enter their flow state’ are magic. \n\nand then the music. justin horowitz struck gold with the soundtrack for this film. the album’s firmly within my top 10 on apple music, and for good reason. there are a lot of upbeat, joyful songs that complement the energy displayed on screen perfectly (the introductory 10/15 minutes are the best example of this), whereas the more peaceful, melodic songs are highlights for me. mia and sebastian’s theme is the peak of the album, but city of stars is a very close second. ryan gosling and emma stone are actually fantastic singers too, before i added the album on apple music i thought it was someone else singing for them\n\nthere’s so many more thoughts in my mind but i’m not good at writing reviews at all so i’ll end this here. this film means so so much to me \n\nalso the ending 15/20 minutes are unapologetically peak cinema. also this is the true winner of best picture \n\n100/100",
